% How we have to write footnotes today
This is a problem%
\footnote:
  This is actually not true.
  See Figure @fig:example.
, but not too bad%
\footnote: This is actually true.
.

% Original design. Issue: [n] might be text the user might want to write.
This is a problem[1], but not too bad[2].
[1]:
  This is actually not true.
  See Figure @fig:example.
[2]: This is actually true.

% Less ambiguous, but maybe hard to keep track of ((n)) vs [[cite]]
This is a problem((1)), but not too bad((2)).
((1)):
  This is actually not true.
  See Figure @fig:example.
((2)): This is actually true.

% Extension of \label/\ref system
% @^number is a temporarily assigned number (can re-use later on)
% @^label uses a label and can be repeated
This is a problem@^1, but not too bad@^false.
@^1 =
  This is actually not true.
  See Figure @fig:example.
@^false = This is actually true.
-> \footnote{\label{false} This is actually true.}

See footnote @false for details.@^false
-> See footnote \ref{false} for details.\footnotemark[\ref{false}]

% Markdown footnotes, combined with above ideas
This is a problem[^1], but not too bad[^false].
[^1]:
  This is actually not true.
  See Figure @fig:example.
[^false]: This is actually true.
-> \footnote{\label{footnote:false} This is actually true.}

See footnote @^false for details.[^false]
-> See footnote \ref{footnote:false} for details.\footnotemark[\ref{footnote:false}]
